N|                 Louise passes the campus center.                                    
O|
N|                 A crowd of STUDENTS are huddled around the glass outside the        
N|                  student center, looking in at a large TV. The crowd is too         
N|                  dense for Louise to see what is on the TV.                         
O|
N|                 Louise frowns, but keeps going.                                     
N|                 Overhead, a second pair of fighter jets rocket past.                
O|
N|                 Louise looks up at the sky. Apprehensive.                           
O|
S|          12     INT. UNIVERSITY CLASSROOM — DAY                     12             
N|                 Louise enters a classroom, leaving the door open behind her.        
N|                 Out in the hall, a couple of STUDENTS run by, but it’s              
N|                 unclear if they’re late for class or it’s something worse.          
O|
N|                 Louise steps to her desk to unload her briefcase and thermos.       
N|                 The course name is written on the blackboard behind the desk:       
N|                 “Advanced Linguistics” and “Dr. Louise Banks.”                     
O|
C|                                   LOUISE                                           
U|                          Good morning, everyone.                                   
O|
N|                 No response. Louise finally looks to the class to discover:         
N|                 Only FOUR STUDENTS seated in an otherwise empty classroom.          
O|
C,E|                                   LOUISE (CONT’D)                                  
U|                          Where is everyone?                                        
O|
O|
O|                                     Pink (05/18/2015)              5.              
O|
N|                  The Grad Students in the room shrug. These are the hardcore        
N|                  learners, with their laptops out and textbooks open.               
N|                  The class session BELL chimes over the PA. Louise looks back       
N|                  at the door, silently considering something, then decides:         
O|
C,E|                                   LOUISE (CONT'D)                                  
U|                          Well, let’s get started. Today                            
U|                          we're talking about Portuguese, and                       
U|                          why it sounds sco different from the                      
U|                          other Romance languages.                                  
N|                  Louise walks to a MAP of western Europe on a rolling easel,        
N|                  parked next to a TV.                                               
C,E|                                   LOUISE (CONT'D)                                  
U|                          The story of Portuguese begins with                       
U|                          the Kingdom of Galicia, in the                            
U|                          middle ages, where the language was                       
U|                          seen as an expression of art. The                         
U|                          way it was written and spoken was                         
U|                          rooted in aesthetics.                                     
N|                  One Grad Student’s phone CHIMES with an alert. Louise pauses,      
N|                  thinking what the other Students are thinking--                    
O|
C,E|                                   LOUISE (CONT’D)                                  
U|                          Any news you want to share?                               
N|                  The Student reading his phone frowns, suddenly nervous.            
O|
C|                                   GRAD STUDENT WITH SMARTPHONE                     
U|                          Uhh, Doctor Banks? Can you turn on                        
U|                          the TV to a news channel?                                 
O|
N|                  Louise grabs her TV remote and turns the set to CNN, to            
N,G|                  reveal AERIAL FOOTAGE from a helicopter, also live, as a           
N|                  REPORTER narrates in a near panic:                                 
C|                                   REPORTER IN HELI (V.0O.)                         
U|                          Are you seeing this!? Oh dear god                         
U|                          what is that noise-- WHAT IS IT--                         
U|                          should we be this close to it?!                           
N,G|                  FOOTAGE shows wilderness, where a STRANGE, OBLONG OBJECT           
N|                  hovers over the tree line. It absorbs sunlight and its             
N|                  dimensions are difficult to grasp -- at times it appears           
N|                  almost concave, poised as if to dig into the planet’s crust.       
O|
N,G|                  Another helicopter edges into view, and suddenly the scale of      
N|                  the object is clear: It’s the size of a massive skyscraper.        
N|                  Bumper text at the bottom of the screen reads:                     
N|                 “STRANGE CRAFT IN MONTANA”                                         
O|
O|
O|                                     Pink (05/18/2015)              6.               
O|
N|                  Amid the WHUFF of the helicopter, a reporter on location is         
N|                  shouting in a voice on verge of a nervous breakdown:                
O|
N|                  The audio cuts out and an ANCHORWOMAN takes over. Cutting to        
N|                  the studio where she pulls her attention to the camera:             
O|
C|                                   ANCHORWOMAN                                       
U|                          The object, uh, apparently touched                         
U|                          down forty minutes ago just north                          
U|                          of I-94, we're, we’re waiting to                           
U|                          hear if this is perhaps an                                 
U|                          experimental vessel or--                                   
N|                  She looks for help off-camera and they cut to:                      
N,G|                  MORE FOOTAGE, closer to the ground. The oblong ship is              
N|                  immense. And seemingly without creases or windows.                  
O|
C,E|                                   ANCHORWOMAN (V.O.)                                
U|                          Hold on, it-- I’m learning that                            
U|                          more objects like this have landed                         
U|                          in as many as eight other locations                        
U|                          around the world. We’re waiting for                        
